{"claim_id":"claim-aristotle-da-3.3-058","claim_text":"There is a third type of perception: that of common sensibles (like movement and magnitude) which accompany incidental objects.","expected_ontology_nodes":["Perception","common sensibles","incidental perception"],"rationale":"Definitional claim subdividing perception into a three-type taxonomy; referents are grounded in the 428b quote's 'common attributes' and 'incidental objects' denotations (Perception as the genus, common sensibles as the type being posited, incidental perception as the contrast class with which it co-occurs), while the 'third type' taxonomic framing and the 'like movement and magnitude' parenthetical examples in claim_text are extractor-supplied beyond the quote.","source":{"author":"Aristotle","title":"De Anima","year":"c. 350 BCE","slug":"aristotle-da-3.3"},"genre":"primary_aristotle","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — ontology has English 'common sensibles' plus Greek slugs 'koina' and 'koina aistheta' for the same concept; labeled the English form per §3.2 decision. | PL:object-of-type-canonicalization — claim_text names 'incidental objects' but the claim's argumentative structure concerns the perceptual type's role (accompanying another type), so canonicalized to 'incidental perception' per §3.2 decision."}
{"claim_id":"claim-aristotle-da-3.3-007","claim_text":"A point, being simultaneously one and two, is properly said to be divisible.","expected_ontology_nodes":["point","unity","divisible"],"rationale":"Thetic claim about a geometric point's simultaneous unity and divisibility; the three referents (point, unity, divisible) are named in claim_text and grounded directly in the 427a1 quote, each passing the §3.0 node-choice principle (concept-match at correct abstraction level, no richer alternative). The analogical targets of the deployment in 3.2/3.3 (Time, discriminating faculty) are flagged as implicit referents rather than labeled; the predicated 'two' has no canonical node at the correct level of abstraction and is flagged AF4.","source":{"author":"Aristotle","title":"De Anima","year":"c. 350 BCE","slug":"aristotle-da-3.3"},"genre":"primary_aristotle","notes":"AF4:two (numerical duality) — no ontology node at the correct level of abstraction for the Aristotelian technical concept of ἀριθμός / πλῆθος; 'number' is broader, 'discrete plurality' / 'numbers' / 'plurality' are all too broad and thin (see drift-log 2026-04-22 slug-graveyard entry for the seven-slug research result). | AF5:Time — analogical target at 427a1; the claim is deployed to illuminate how the time of discrimination (a moment) is simultaneously one and two hence divisible, but Time is not named in claim_text. | AF4:discriminating-faculty — no richly-canonical node; 'krisis', 'kritikon', 'to kritikon', 'discrimination', 'judgement' all exist only as thin slugs that are also broader than the Aristotelian τὸ κρῖνον technical sense. | AF5:discriminating-faculty — analogical target at 427a1; the point's one-and-two-hence-divisible structure illuminates how a single discriminating faculty can grasp contraries without losing unity, but the faculty is not named in claim_text."}
{"claim_id":"claim-aristotle-da-3.3-033","claim_text":"When we merely imagine something fearful or encouraging, we remain emotionally unaffected, like viewers of a painting.","expected_ontology_nodes":["Imagination","Emotion"],"rationale":"Empirical observation relating two psychological powers: under mere imagination (without doxastic commitment), no emotional affection is produced. Imagination and Emotion are the referents named in claim_text, each a rich canonical grounded in the 427b22 quote; the painting is an illustrative analogy and not a referent; the implicit contrast with opinion/doxa (the judgmental take-as-true state that does produce emotion, per nearby claim-032) is flagged AF5 rather than labeled under the strict-surface rule.","source":{"author":"Aristotle","title":"De Anima","year":"c. 350 BCE","slug":"aristotle-da-3.3"},"genre":"primary_aristotle","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — ontology has rich canonicals Imagination (φαντασία) and Emotion (πάθος) alongside thin slugs (phantasia; pathos, affection) for the same two concepts; labeled the rich English forms per §3.2 decision. | AF5:opinion — elided contrast supplied by 427b22 context via 'merely imagine' (the contrasted state is taking-something-to-be-fearful, i.e. the doxa/judgment state that does produce emotion per nearby claim-032); held back from expected_ontology_nodes under strict-surface rule. The 'opinion' slot is thin and sits within a 13-slug opinion/doxa/hupolepsis concept-cluster with no rich canonical among them; see drift-log 2026-04-22 for the second slug-graveyard instance counted against the item-007 pattern-watch."}
{"claim_id":"claim-aristotle-da-3.3-053","claim_text":"Actual sensation can produce a movement that is necessarily similar in character to the sensation itself.","expected_ontology_nodes":["Perception","Actuality","Movement/Change"],"rationale":"Thetic claim at 428b1 stating a causal relation that functions as part of the definition of phantasia: actual (energized) sensation produces a movement (= phantasia, per the definitional frame established at claim 052) that is necessarily similar in character to the sensation itself. Surface referents in claim_text are Perception (the active sensation as cause), Actuality (the energeia qualifier distinguishing active from potential sensation, load-bearing for the claim), and Movement/Change (the produced kinesis). Imagination (phantasia) is flagged AF5 as the claim-internal implicit referent: the indefinite article 'a movement' functions as definite reference to the specific kind of movement the passage is defining, though phantasia itself is not named in this claim_text.","source":{"author":"Aristotle","title":"De Anima","year":"c. 350 BCE","slug":"aristotle-da-3.3"},"genre":"primary_aristotle","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — three synonymy cases in this one claim: (a) Perception (rich, αἴσθησις) + aisthesis (thin slug) for the same concept, English form labeled per §3.2 decision. (b) Movement/Change (rich, no-spaces, DA-emphasized def including 'phantasia defined as a kinesis') + Movement / Change (rich, with-spaces, Physics-emphasized def distinguishing kinesis from energeia) + kinesis (thin slug) — two rich canonicals for the same concept; labeled the no-spaces variant because its DA-specific definition matches the 428b1 passage context per §3.0 (rich-preferred-by-context-match). (c) Actuality (rich, ἐνέργεια) + Actuality / Fulfilment (rich, engages energeia-vs-entelecheia refinement) + three thin slugs (energeia, actuality (ἐνεργείᾳ), actuality (ἐντελέχεια)) — labeled Actuality because the claim deploys the energeia-vs-dunamis contrast without engaging the energeia-vs-entelecheia refinement. | AF5:Imagination — claim-internal implicit referent; the indefinite 'a movement' functions as definite reference to phantasia in the definitional context of 428b1 (cf. claim 052: 'imagination is held to be a movement and to be impossible without sensation'). Imagination has a rich canonical (φαντασία) but is not named in claim_text, so flagged AF5 rather than labeled under strict-surface."}
{"claim_id":"claim-aristotle-da-3.3-037","claim_text":"If actual imagination were identical to actual sensation, imagination would exist in all animals, but it is not thought to.","expected_ontology_nodes":["Imagination","Perception","Actuality","brutes"],"rationale":"Dialectical refutation (reductio) at 428a8: if actual imagination and actual sensation were identical, imagination would universally accompany sensation and thus be present in all brutes; the empirical observation that ants, bees, and grubs lack imagination (per nearby claim-038) refutes the antecedent identity thesis. Referents are Imagination and Perception (the two faculties whose identity is being denied), Actuality (the energeia qualifier distinguishing active from potential states, load-bearing for the specific form of the thesis — Aristotle is not refuting identity of the mere capacities), and brutes (the class over which the first consequent universally quantifies; claim_text 'animals' is a paraphrase broadening of Greek θηρία, which specifically means non-rational animals — labeled brutes per §3.0 concept-match, since Aristotle's empirical counter-evidence applies only to non-rational animals). The 'identity' relation operates structurally in the reductio but is not a posited referent and not labeled. No AF5 flag: the reductio's implicit warrant (universal-over-animals inference) and implicit empirical fact are argumentative propositions, not conceptual implicit-referents.","source":{"author":"Aristotle","title":"De Anima","year":"c. 350 BCE","slug":"aristotle-da-3.3"},"genre":"primary_aristotle","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — four synonymy cases in this one claim: (a) Perception (rich, αἴσθησις) + aisthesis (thin slug); (b) Imagination (rich, φαντασία) + phantasia (thin slug); (c) Actuality (rich, ἐνέργεια) + three thin variants (energeia, actuality (ἐνεργείᾳ), actuality (ἐντελέχεια)) — labeled Actuality as at item 053 because the claim deploys the energeia-vs-dunamis contrast without engaging the entelecheia refinement; (d) animals (thin, claim_text surface) + brutes (thin, source-Greek concept θηρία) — labeled brutes per §3.0 concept-match because claim_text 'animals' is a paraphrase-broadening and Aristotle's actual argument targets non-rational animals specifically (ants/bees/grubs per claim-038). Observation: the ontology's 'animal'-prefixed namespace contains 15+ thin-slug entries for related-but-distinct concepts (animal activity, animal cognition, animal soul, animalia imperfecta, etc.) — does NOT count as a slug-graveyard instance because the sibling nodes denote distinct concepts rather than the same concept, but worth noting for upstream ontology curation."}
{"claim_id":"claim-aristotle-da-3.3-039","claim_text":"Sensations are always true, while imaginations are mostly false.","expected_ontology_nodes":["Perception","Imagination","true","false"],"rationale":"Dialectical premise at 428a11 endorsed by Aristotle (per source-record stance) asserting an asymmetric truth-value distribution across two perceptual faculties: sensations are always true, imaginations are mostly false. The four referents (Perception, Imagination, true, false) are all named in claim_text as the subjects and predicates of the parallel contrastive predications. The claim is deployed as a premise in the larger reductio refuting the phantasia=aisthesis identity thesis (per source-record warrant: 'if sensation and imagination have different truth-values, they cannot be identical'), but the identity thesis is passage-level argumentative purpose, not claim-internal content — no AF5 flag here; compare item 053 where phantasia WAS AF5-flagged because the claim was itself a move IN the definition of phantasia.","source":{"author":"Aristotle","title":"De Anima","year":"c. 350 BCE","slug":"aristotle-da-3.3"},"genre":"primary_aristotle","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — four synonymy cases: (a) Perception (rich, αἴσθησις) + aisthesis (thin slug); (b) Imagination (rich, φαντασία) + phantasia (thin slug); (c) true (thin, English adjective) + alethes (thin, Greek transliteration) — no Aristotelian rich canonical available for this concept; labeled the English-adjective surface form per §3.2 decision; (d) false (thin, English adjective) + falsehood (thin, English noun) + pseudh (thin, Greek transliteration) — labeled 'false' per surface-match to claim_text adjective and §3.2 English-form preference. Observation — wrong-context rich canonical: the ontology has a rich 'Truth' node but its definition is explicitly Heideggerian (aletheia-as-unconcealment per Being and Time), not Aristotelian correspondence-truth; labeling 'Truth' here would be mis-denotation under §3.0 concept-match. Registered as pattern-watch wrong-context-rich-canonical in drift-log 2026-04-22, with retrospective observations for item 033 (Fear rejected as Heideggerian Furcht) and item 037 (animal rejected as Heideggerian-context slug)."}
{"claim_id":"claim-heidegger-bcap-091","claim_text":"The human being's being-in-the-world is fundamentally determined through speaking","expected_ontology_nodes":["Dasein","Being-in-the-world","Discourse"],"rationale":"Thetic claim asserting Heidegger's view that speaking is constitutive of Dasein's fundamental structural whole (being-in-the-world), not an add-on capability. The three referents are all rich Heideggerian canonicals: Dasein (the entity we ourselves are; German 'Dasein'), Being-in-the-world (In-der-Welt-sein, Dasein's basic state), and Discourse (Rede, the third equiprimordial existentiale of disclosedness — more primordial than worldly language). Claim_text uses 'human being' as Heidegger's ordinary-language surface for Dasein and 'speaking' as his ordinary-language surface for Rede; both canonicalize to their rich technical nodes per §3.0 concept-match and §3.2 ontology-synonymy (rich preferred), with the new §3.0 rich-vs-strict tension corollary supporting the default (this claim's deployment engages the Dasein-specific structure of being-in-the-world, not a generic human-being claim). No AF5 for Aristotelian λόγος: the BCAP-wide retrieval of λόγος via Rede is passage-level argumentative purpose, not claim-internal content (cf. item 037's no-AF5 on phantasia=aisthesis identity; contrast item 053's AF5 where the claim was itself one move in the definition of phantasia).","source":{"author":"Heidegger, Martin","title":"Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy","year":"2009","slug":"heidegger-bcap"},"genre":"primary_heidegger","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — two cases: (a) human being (thin) + Dasein (rich, German 'Dasein') — labeled Dasein per §3.2 rich-preferred; (b) speaking (thin) + Discourse (rich, German 'Rede') — labeled Discourse per same rule. First primary_heidegger item; rich Heideggerian canonicals here are concept-correct, see drift-log 2026-04-22 for framework-validity note on the wrong-context-rich-canonical pattern."}
{"claim_id":"claim-heidegger-bcap-079","claim_text":"For the Greeks, all speaking is speaking to or with others or oneself","expected_ontology_nodes":["Logos"],"rationale":"Exegetical claim asserting Heidegger's reading of the Greek concept of λόγος (λέγειν): Greek speech is inherently relational/addressed — not a neutral declarative act but always speaking-to-someone (others or oneself). Single content-referent: Logos (the Aristotelian rich canonical for the Greek concept being interpreted; labeled over the also-rich λόγος node per §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match because Logos's polysemic def covers 'speech' explicitly while λόγος's def is Rhetoric-specific). 'For the Greeks' is attribution-scope rather than a content-referent: the claim's content is about λόγος's structure, not about the Greeks themselves as an object of discussion — Greeks/Greek/ancients slugs exist but are not labeled here under the attribution-scope-not-referent reading (new pattern-watch registered in drift-log 2026-04-22 with 079 as triggering case). The relational addressees the claim's structure requires — addressee-others and reflexive-oneself — both lack correct-level-of-abstraction ontology nodes: the 'others' slug is Heidegger-introduced but at the broader Mitsein-Others scope rather than the claim-specific addressee-others scope (per §3.0 reject-broader-than-claim), and no ontology node exists for the Greek reflexive-addressee concept; both flagged AF4. No AF5 for Being-with/Mitsein: Heidegger's interpretive lens is passage-level argumentative context, not claim-internal implicit referent (cf. item 037, not item 053). Context-sensitive canonicalization: 'speaking' here maps to Logos (exegetical-about-Greeks) rather than Discourse (as in item 091's Dasein-assertive context) — the claim's argumentative mode drives the canonicalization target; registered as a separate pattern-watch in drift-log 2026-04-22.","source":{"author":"Heidegger, Martin","title":"Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy","year":"2009","slug":"heidegger-bcap"},"genre":"primary_heidegger","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — (a) Logos (rich, English-name Aristotelian polysemic) + λόγος (rich, Greek-character Aristotelian, Rhetoric-specific def) for the same Greek concept; labeled Logos per §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match. (b) speaking (thin, Heidegger-introduced) is context-sensitive: canonicalizes to Discourse in 091 (Heideggerian-assertive-about-Dasein) but to Logos here (exegetical-about-Greeks); registered as separate pattern-watch in drift-log 2026-04-22. (c) Greeks / Greek / ancients thin-slug cluster for overlapping collective-attribution concepts — not labeled here under the attribution-scope-not-referent reading (new pattern-watch registered). | AF4:others — Heideggerian Mitsein-Others concept exists as thin slug but is broader than the claim-specific addressee-others scope; per §3.0 reject-broader-than-claim, no correct-level-of-abstraction node available. | AF4:oneself — no ontology node for the Greek reflexive-addressee concept; adjacent nodes (Selfhood, self-*) too broad or off-target. Observations: 'For the Greeks' treated as attribution-scope rather than content-referent (new pattern-watch, triggering case); 'speaking' context-sensitive canonicalization between Discourse (091) and Logos (079) (new pattern-watch registered). Third instance of rich-rich ontology-synonymy duplication (Logos vs λόγος; cf. Movement/Change and Actuality variants at 053) — governance-threshold count but already covered by §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match corollary; drift-log trace entry only, no new pattern-watch."}
{"claim_id":"claim-heidegger-bcap-036","claim_text":"What the concept itself is in its conceptuality is not yet visible.","expected_ontology_nodes":["concept","conceptuality","Disclosedness"],"rationale":"Aporetic methodological claim asserting that the essence of a concept (its conceptuality, Begrifflichkeit) has not yet been phenomenologically disclosed. Three referents: concept (thin Heidegger-introduced slug; no richer canonical for the Begriff concept-territory), conceptuality (thin Heidegger-introduced slug; no richer canonical for Begrifflichkeit), and Disclosedness (rich Heideggerian canonical with German Erschlossenheit — the 'there' of Dasein as clearing in which entities can show themselves). Labeled Disclosedness over the thin visibility slug per §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match: the surrounding passage constructs an explicit contrast between definitional-explicitness ('the concept becomes explicit') and phenomenological-disclosure ('still... not yet visible'), establishing 'visible' as hermeneutic-disclosure (Erschlossenheit) rather than colloquial epistemic access. The three-step methodological program that immediately follows (originally-seen → primarily-addressed → spoken-into-conceptuality) confirms the disclosure-theoretic register. No AF5: the 'not yet' temporal marker carries a methodological-task implication but no claim-internal implicit referent. No AF4: each surface concept has a matching canonical (either thin-with-no-alternative or rich). No attribution-scope case.","source":{"author":"Heidegger, Martin","title":"Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy","year":"2009","slug":"heidegger-bcap"},"genre":"primary_heidegger","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — one case: visibility (thin, Heidegger-introduced) + Disclosedness (rich, Erschlossenheit) for the hermeneutic-visibility concept the claim deploys; labeled Disclosedness per §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match (explicit/disclosure contrast and subsequent phenomenological-program signal technical hermeneutic visibility, not ordinary perceptual visibility). concept and conceptuality are thin slugs without richer canonical alternatives (no Begriff/Begrifflichkeit rich nodes) — labeled directly per §3.0 criterion (a)+(b). Observation: nearby_provenance is empty for this claim (unlike other BCAP items anchored to Aristotelian Bekker numbers) — a methodological aporetic not commenting on a specific Aristotle passage; see drift-log 2026-04-22 for second novel-provenance situation for Heidegger material (first at item 091). Observation: third consecutive accept of technical-upgrade on primary_heidegger material (091 speaking→Discourse; 079 speaking→Logos; 036 visible→Disclosedness) — see drift-log 2026-04-22 heidegger-technical-upgrade-pattern trace."}
{"claim_id":"claim-heidegger-bcap-066","claim_text":"Proprium is that one thing which is predicated of many things with respect to a necessary quality that lies outside the essential context of genus and species.","expected_ontology_nodes":["proprium","genus","species","Essence"],"rationale":"Definitional claim (mention/reports_neutral) stating the scholastic-Aristotelian definition of proprium as one of the five predicables. §5.4 partial-faithfulness verification performed: source record's `quote` field captures only the Latin portion ('Proprium est unum, quod de pluribus praedicatur in quale necessarium'), omitting the English gloss 'lies outside of the essential context of genus and species' that appears in Heidegger's §5(a) text immediately following the Latin. The claim_text combines Latin-translation and immediately-following English gloss, both of which are Heidegger's own text. Under §3.2 paraphrase-distortion criterion (labels-from-quote vs labels-from-claim): the extractor's `faithfulness: partial` flag is warranted by the quote-truncation, but the label set derived from the full Heidegger passage matches the claim_text labels → elaboration, not distortion. No PL:paraphrase-distortion flag. Four referents (conservative set under Option B): proprium (the defined concept), genus and species (contrast classes the definition excludes proprium from), and Essence (rich Aristotelian canonical τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, for 'essential context' — labeled over the thin `essential` slug per §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match). Structural components of the definition-form (unity from 'est unum'; necessary quality from 'in quale necessarium') are NOT labeled under the conservative reading — they are constitutive of the predicable-definition genre shared across all five predicables, not distinguishing content of proprium specifically. Predicables, predication, and differentia are passage-level classificatory context, not claim-internal content; no AF5 flag (parallel to items 037 and 079, not 053).","source":{"author":"Heidegger, Martin","title":"Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy","year":"2009","slug":"heidegger-bcap"},"genre":"primary_heidegger","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — one case: essential (adjective-form surface) + Essence (rich, τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι) for the essential-context concept; labeled Essence per §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match. The other labeled concepts (proprium, genus, species) are thin slugs without richer canonical alternatives. §5.4 partial-faithfulness verification: quote-truncation (extractor captured only Latin, not the English gloss that follows in Heidegger's text) warrants the 'partial' flag but not paraphrase-distortion — label set derived from full Heidegger passage matches claim_text labels, so this is elaboration. No PL:paraphrase-distortion flag. First §5.4 activation on a committed item (items 1 through 9 were all supported); see drift-log 2026-04-22 s54-procedure-first-activation trace. Observation: fourth consecutive accept of technical-upgrade on primary_heidegger material (essential→Essence; cf. 091 speaking→Discourse, 079 speaking→Logos, 036 visible→Disclosedness) — pattern-watch now formally registered as heidegger-technical-upgrade-default in drift-log 2026-04-22 with 4-observation pre-population and first-rejection trigger."}
{"claim_id":"claim-papachristou-2013-127","claim_text":"Aristotle characterizes phantasia as a kind of motion (κίνησις) in the soul that cannot exist apart from sensation and is caused by the activity of sensation.","expected_ontology_nodes":["Imagination","Movement/Change","Soul","Perception"],"rationale":"Papachristou paraphrasing Aristotle's iconic 428b11-13 phantasia-as-motion definition. Four surface referents — phantasia, motion (κίνησις), the soul, sensation — each canonicalize to a rich Aristotelian node: Imagination (rich English canonical for phantasia per §3.2 ontology-synonymy English-form rule, parallel to 033/037/053), Movement/Change (rich no-spaces variant per §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match matching 053's choice for the same DA-3.3 definitional cluster; alternates Movement / Change with-spaces, movement thin, kinesis thin), Soul (rich English canonical per §3.2 since claim_text uses English 'the soul' with no Greek-glyph in claim_text; alternates ψυχή rich Greek-glyph and psyche thin transliteration — first tri-form synonymy instance, see drift-log entry), and Perception (rich English canonical for sensation per §3.2; alternate aisthesis thin slug). 'The activity of sensation' is NOT labeled Actuality despite the structural parallel to item 053's 'actual sensation' → Actuality: the energeia/dunamis specification is doctrinal underpinning of phantasia's dependence on actual rather than potential sensation, but 'activity of sensation' is reportorial-descriptive rather than surface-deploying the actual-vs-potential contrast (cf. 053's 'actual' as adjective directly modifying sensation). Flagged AF5:Actuality to record the implicit doctrinal referent under strict-surface labeling. 'Aristotle characterizes' is treated as attribution-scope rather than content-referent: the claim's content is about phantasia's structure as Papachristou reports Aristotle, not about Aristotle as an object of discussion (second instance of attribution-scope-not-referent pattern-watch registered at 079; observation logged in drift-log; observational-not-preventive on this case since Aristotle was not a candidate label up for consideration). Genre note: first secondary_phantasia item; the four rich Aristotelian canonicals labeled here all match choices made on primary_aristotle items 058/007/033/053/037/039 for the same concepts — confirms cross-genre framework-validity for the rich-preferred-on-Aristotelian-material rule (drift-log trace with suffixed token rich-preferred-cross-genre-confirmation, parallel to 091's wrong-context-rich-canonical-framework-validity entry but for the inverse case). Tri-form synonymy on Soul vs ψυχή vs psyche is structurally novel relative to 053/079 bi-form duplications and warrants its own pattern-watch candidate (separate drift-log entry).","source":{"author":"Papachristou, Christina","title":"Three Kinds or Grades of Phantasia in Aristotle's De Anima","year":"2013","slug":"papachristou-2013"},"genre":"secondary_phantasia","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — four synonymy cases in this one claim: (a) Imagination (rich, English) + phantasia (thin Greek-transliteration) for the same concept; English form labeled per §3.2 (parallel to 033/037/053). (b) Perception (rich, αἴσθησις) + aisthesis (thin) for the same concept; English form labeled per §3.2 (claim_text uses 'sensation' English). (c) Movement/Change (rich, no-spaces, DA-emphasized def) + Movement / Change (rich, with-spaces, Physics-emphasized def) + movement (thin) + kinesis (thin) — two rich + two thin; labeled Movement/Change per §3.0 rich-preferred-by-context-match matching 053's choice for the same DA-3.3 cluster. (d) Soul (rich, English canonical) + ψυχή (rich, Greek-glyph) + psyche (thin transliteration) — first tri-form synonymy instance (cf. bi-form 053 Movement, 053 Actuality, 079 Logos); resolved by collapsing thin first (eliminating psyche), then applying §3.0 between Soul and ψυχή with English-form preference per §3.2 — net pick Soul. Pattern-watch candidate registered separately in drift-log. | AF5:Actuality — the energeia/dunamis specification is doctrinal underpinning of phantasia's dependence on actual rather than potential sensation, but 'activity of sensation' is reportorial-descriptive rather than surface-deploying the actual-vs-potential contrast (distinct from 053 'actual sensation' where 'actual' was adjective directly modifying sensation). Flagged under strict-surface labeling per AF5 slot-semantics. Observations: 'Aristotle characterizes' treated as attribution-scope rather than content-referent (second instance of attribution-scope-not-referent pattern-watch registered at 079; observation logged in drift-log; observational-not-preventive — Aristotle is not in this item's `_candidate_metadata.key_concepts`, so the flag records the pattern instance for trigger counting without rejecting a candidate up for consideration; distinguished from the preventive-rejection evidentiary type). Tri-form synonymy on Soul / ψυχή / psyche is the first three-way duplication observed (separate drift-log entry as pattern-watch candidate). First secondary_phantasia item — four rich Aristotelian canonicals matching primary_aristotle choices confirm cross-genre framework-validity (drift-log trace observation — rich-preferred-cross-genre-confirmation, parallel to 091's wrong-context-rich-canonical-framework-validity entry but for the inverse case: rich Aristotelian canonicals correct on both primary and secondary Aristotelian material)."}
{"claim_id":"claim-white-1985-069","claim_text":"Aristotle's analysis of phantasia has implications that extend to among the most significant and original conclusions of his psychology.","expected_ontology_nodes":["Imagination"],"rationale":"Meta-textual evaluation by White locating phantasia analysis within Aristotle's broader De Anima project: the analysis has implications that reach to the most significant conclusions of his psychology (= his investigation of the soul; scholarly-synonym substitution in the paraphrase, see drift-log s32-distortion-criterion-edge-case). The claim is evaluative-meta about the place of phantasia analysis in Aristotle's project, not a substantive claim about phantasia, soul, or psychology-as-discipline. Single labelable referent: Imagination (rich English canonical for phantasia per §3.2 ontology-synonymy English-form rule, parallel to 033/037/053/127). 'his psychology' is meta-disciplinary frame, not a denotable content concept — does not name a substantive co-referent at the correct level of abstraction (psychology slug exists but is Caston-introduced thin-only with no def beyond author-attribution; Soul exists rich but is the subject Aristotle investigates, not 'his psychology' itself, so labeling Soul would read through the paraphrase per §3.2 prohibition). 'implications', 'significant', 'original', 'conclusions' are meta-descriptors. Possessive attribution ('Aristotle's analysis', 'his psychology') registered as new pattern-watch possessive-attribution-scope in drift-log, separate from the verbal/prepositional attribution-scope-not-referent watch (079) to preserve joint-evaluation at item-25; observational-not-preventive on this case since neither Aristotle nor a possessive-owned candidate concept was up for consideration. No PL:paraphrase-distortion despite paraphrase substituting 'his psychology' for source quote's 'investigation of the soul' — scholarly-synonym in Aristotelian context (psychology ≈ peri psychēs), not concept-shift; logged as §3.2 row-refinement candidate in drift-log s32-distortion-criterion-edge-case.","source":{"author":"White, Kevin","title":"The Meaning of Phantasia in Aristotle's De Anima III, 3-8","year":"1985","slug":"white-1985"},"genre":"secondary_phantasia","notes":"PL:ontology-synonymy — one case: Imagination (rich, English) + phantasia (thin Greek-transliteration) for the same concept; English form labeled per §3.2 (parallel to 033/037/053/127). Observation: possessive-attribution form ('Aristotle's analysis', 'his psychology') is structurally distinct from the prepositional-scope (079: 'For the Greeks') and verbal-attribution (127: 'Aristotle characterizes') forms — the possessor is grammatical owner of the claim's subject, integrating attribution more tightly with the claim's content. Registered as new pattern-watch possessive-attribution-scope (drift-log 2026-04-22), separate from attribution-scope-not-referent watch (079) to preserve joint-evaluation at item-25. Observational-not-preventive: Aristotle is not in `_candidate_metadata.key_concepts` and 'his psychology' / 'Aristotle's analysis' are not candidate labels; possessive structure logged as registration instance rather than rejection of a candidate. Meta-textual claim structure: 'his psychology' functions as meta-disciplinary frame (= scope-marker for what project the analysis belongs to), not as a substantive co-referent denoting a labelable concept; 'implications', 'conclusions', 'significant', 'original' are meta-descriptors. No AF5 — no implicit substantive referent under the rule's slot-semantics; the un-labeled material is meta-textual evaluation, not a concept-implicit-but-not-named in the AF5 sense (cf. 037 distinction: claim-internal implicit referent vs passage-level argumentative content). No PL:paraphrase-distortion despite paraphrase's 'his psychology' for source quote's 'investigation of the soul' — scholarly-synonym substitution in Aristotelian context, not concept-shift; logged as §3.2 criterion-edge-case in drift-log for item-25 refinement consideration."}
